      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Forest_Village%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Its design pioneered gracefully curved streets and tree retention, moving away from straight grids typical post‑WWII suburbs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Forest_Village%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Park Forest Village was developed on 1,000 acres of the Barrens in Patton Township by J.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Forest_Village%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Alvin Hawbaker and assistant Carl W. Wild created the cul‑de‑sac and curved street layout that increased housing density.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Forest_Village%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Each homesite kept as many native trees as possible, giving properties a mature, long‑term look unlike newer developments.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Park_Forest_Village%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Local parks were placed so every neighborhood had a nearby recreation area, a pattern that influenced future developments.",
